{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-130539",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The probability density function for a uniformly distributed continuous random variable $X$ on an interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{b-a},\\, & a\\le x\\le b, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur random variable $X$ is uniformly distributed on the interval $[-4,0],$ so it has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4},\\, & -4\\le x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<-\\sqrt{2}) & =P(-4\\le X<-\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{-\\sqrt{2}}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{-\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}-(-4)}{4} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
